Howdy Next for Debian
=====================

This is an unofficial personal package. It does not enable PAM on
install. Test recognition before changing authentication.

After install
-------------

1. Identify a stable camera path under /dev/v4l/by-id/ or
   /dev/v4l/by-path/.
2. Run: sudo howdy config
   Set [video] device_path to that camera.
3. Run: sudo howdy download-models
   (uses FP32 SFace; INT8 fails under OpenCV 5's graph engine)
4. Run: sudo howdy add
5. Run: sudo howdy test

After upgrading from 3.4.0-4 or earlier, download models again and
re-enroll faces. INT8 and FP32 embeddings are not interchangeable.

PAM
---

Only add pam_howdy.so after "howdy test" works. See pam_howdy(8) and
https://codeberg.org/nathawat/howdy-next/wiki/PAM-Integration

Typical common-auth line, above pam_unix (do not lock yourself
out; keep a password fallback). This also covers sudo and
polkit-1; do not duplicate it in those files:

  auth  sufficient  pam_howdy.so

Omit workaround=native and workaround=native-input. Those flags
break sudo-rs and KDE polkit.

polkit 127 sandboxes polkit-agent-helper@.service, which hides
/dev/video* and /dev/uinput. Helper stderr also inherits the
protocol socket, so OpenCV warnings make KDE cancel the dialog.
This package installs a systemd drop-in that opens those devices
and sets StandardError=journal. See
https://codeberg.org/nathawat/howdy-next/wiki/Polkit-127-compatibility

The setuid helper is /usr/lib/howdy/howdy-auth-helper. PAM compare
is /usr/lib/howdy/howdy-compare. sudo howdy test does not exercise
those paths. /etc/howdy is root-only; that is intentional.

Vendored OpenCV
---------------

Debian does not yet ship OpenCV 5 in main. This package includes private OpenCV 5 libraries under
/usr/lib/howdy-next so it does not conflict with Debian's OpenCV 4.
